# Optional: the status page at a host name of your own. Exposing it is the
# operator's choice. The page writes nothing and takes no action, but it shows
# everything in the queue: each request's text, every instruction and output,
# the diffs, the configuration as read. Its own protection is HTTP basic
# authentication (secrets.yaml.example); /healthz alone answers without it,
# for a load balancer's health check. Without this object the page is still
# reachable by port-forward (SETUP.md, "Checking that it is up").
#
# Written for any Ingress controller. A cloud load balancer controller usually
# takes the certificate, the scheme and the health check path from its own
# annotations instead of spec.tls: point that health check at /healthz. If you
# restrict the source addresses, test the restriction from an address outside
# it: an annotation the controller does not apply is ignored without an error.
#
# The Pod's egress rules reject private destinations (egress-configmap.yaml.example).
# A load balancer reaches the Pod from a private address, so the replies are
# allowed only by the rule for source port 9200; without that rule the replies
# are dropped and the load balancer answers 504 (this was met in practice).
# Egress rules are applied when the Pod starts, so a change to them needs a
# restart.
#
# Once the page is reachable here, set intake.status_page in the operator
# configuration to https://<status-host>/jobs/ so the acceptance comment links
# to the request's own page.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: <consumer>-ticket-engine-status
  namespace: <namespace>
spec:
  ingressClassName: <ingress-class>
  tls:
    - hosts: [<status-host>]
      secretName: <status-tls-secret>
  rules:
    - host: <status-host>
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: <consumer>-ticket-engine-status
                port:
                  number: 9200
